__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.venv/
build/
dist/
# generated data (regenerate from configs / re-drop per docs/LOCAL_DATA_SETUP.md)
data/cache/
data/three_gene_dataset/*.h5
data/datasets/*/payload.h5
data/staging/

# RUN RECORDS ARE TRACKED. `experiments/` used to be ignored wholesale, which meant the
# audit trail behind every number in docs/ was absent from version control: you could read
# a claim but not the run that produced it. This project is headed for a paper, so the
# evidence is versioned alongside the claims. It is affordable because the model is tiny
# (3N^2+3N parameters): a whole run directory is ~15 KB and the run index ~5 KB per 30 runs.
#
# RECOVERED MODELS ARE TRACKED TOO. A checkpoint is ~3 KB here (the model has 3N^2+3N
# parameters), and it is what `rngrn evaluate --run-id ...` reloads to re-simulate a
# recovered network. results/train_results.json carries the same parameters as text for
# reading and citing; the .pt carries them in the form you can RUN. Both, deliberately:
# text survives a torch upgrade, the checkpoint reproduces the dynamics exactly.
#
# THE PLOTTABLE ARRAYS ARE TRACKED TOO (unit P1, docs/DECISIONS.md D-PLOT-1). Scalars audit
# a number; ARRAYS draw a figure, and none of the figures a paper on inverse Turing patterns
# needs — the pattern, the dispersion relation, the spectrum, the learning curve — can be
# drawn from runs.jsonl. `arrays/*.npz` is therefore version-controlled with the claims it
# supports. MEASURED cost: 110 KB (no history, no model field) to 645 KB (the full phase-C
# shape: target frame + rolled-out field + sigma(k) recovered and true + both RAPS + the
# thinned training trajectory), i.e. ~10-62 MB for a 96-run phase-C wave, and 0.82 MB per
# cross-L evaluation. That is affordable; a bare
# `*.npz` rule was not the reason it was excluded before — nothing was writing one.
#
#   tracked  — runs.jsonl / target_reports.jsonl / lgen_eval.jsonl / lgen_summary.jsonl
#              (the append-only indexes),
#              config/frozen_config.yaml (the config that ACTUALLY ran),
#              results/*.json (metrics, provenance, recovered parameters + Jacobian),
#              checkpoints/*.pt (the runnable recovered model),
#              arrays/*.npz (the PLOTTABLE arrays: fields, spectra, sigma(k), trajectory),
#              exp*.json / exp*.csv (the experiment-script evidence ledger)
#   ignored  — only regenerable bulk: figures (redrawn from arrays/ in seconds by
#              scripts/make_figures.py), HDF5 dumps, bare .npy, and any npz OUTSIDE
#              arrays/ (which by convention is a scratch dump, not a run artefact).
experiments/**/figures/
experiments/**/*.h5
experiments/**/*.npz
!experiments/**/arrays/*.npz
experiments/**/*.npy
experiments/**/*.png

# ...WITH ONE EXCEPTION. `experiments/figures_report/` holds REPORT figures, where the
# IMAGE is the deliverable rather than a regenerable view of an array — the Stage-0
# biological-viability check exists because the owner asked to "visually verify ... that
# our model does in fact form turing patterns under the biological conditions", and a
# boolean in a JSON file does not answer that. These PNGs are therefore tracked, together
# with the arrays/ behind every panel (already tracked by the rule above), so the figure
# and its data are auditable as a pair. This is deliberately NOT a global un-ignore: the
# per-run sweep figures must stay regenerable from arrays/ by scripts/make_figures.py, or
# a 96-run wave would add ~100 MB of PNGs to the repo.
!experiments/figures_report/**/*.png

# tidy CSV exports (`rngrn export`) — a pure projection of the tracked indexes
exports/

# downloaded literature (not ours to redistribute)
papers/
